<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.1" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="42349-1" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Reason for referral (narrative)"/>
    <title>Reason for Referral</title>
    <text><paragraph>Iron deficiency anemia with three months of looser stools and dark rectal bleeding in a man of 64; colorectal cancer suspected. Colonoscopy and staging CT are booked. Please see him once they are done to review the stage, the order of treatment, and the probable need for adjuvant therapy. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Urgency: Urgent suspected-cancer pathway, within three weeks; colonoscopy booked within eight days. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Questions for the consultant:</paragraph><list listType="ordered"><item>Once colonoscopy and CT are complete, what is the stage, and should he have surgery first or treatment before surgery?</item><item>Does anything on staging need further imaging before an operation?</item><item>Should his iron deficiency be corrected before surgery, and how?</item><item>If adjuvant chemotherapy is expected, what should be done beforehand, and what would its duration be?</item><item>Does his family history call for genetic assessment?</item></list></text>

  </section>
</component>

<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.4"/>
    <code code="10164-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Present illness Narrative"/>
    <title>History of Present Illness</title>
    <text><paragraph>Mr. Hume is seen at the request of Dr. Gallacher three weeks after presenting with looser stools, dark rectal bleeding, tiredness, and iron deficiency anemia. Colonoscopy thirteen days ago found a 4 cm ulcerated, non-obstructing mass at 28 cm in the sigmoid colon and no other lesions; the biopsy shows moderately differentiated adenocarcinoma. He still has two or three loose stools a day with some dark blood, and he tires on hills. He has no abdominal pain, obstructive symptoms, cough, or bone pain. He is independent, writes every morning, and wants plain information. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Historical basis. These claims are historical evidence, not findings from a modern examination.</paragraph><list><item>David Hume was born at Edinburgh on April 26, 1711, Old Style (May 7, 1711, in the Gregorian calendar), the second son of Joseph Hume of Ninewells in Berwickshire. [D]</item><item>In My Own Life Hume wrote that his father died when he was an infant, leaving him, an elder brother, and a sister in their mother&apos;s care, and that his mother had died by the time he went to live at his brother&apos;s country house in 1749. [D]</item><item>In a letter to a physician written about 1734, Hume described an illness beginning about September 1729, when he was eighteen: loss of his ardour for study, scurvy spots on his fingers, and from about April 1730 a watery mouth, which his physician called &apos;the disease of the learned&apos;; he was treated with bitters, anti-hysteric pills, a pint of claret a day, and riding, and in 1731 had a ravenous appetite and palpitations. [D]</item><item>In My Own Life, dated April 18, 1776, Hume wrote that in spring 1775 he was struck with a disorder in his bowels, which at first gave him no alarm but which he came to judge mortal and incurable; he wrote that he had suffered very little pain and that his spirits had never abated. [D]</item></list><paragraph>6 later historical claims are omitted because they describe events after the index age. See the teaching note.</paragraph></text>

  </section>
</component>

