<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.8"/>
    <code code="51848-0" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation note"/>
    <title>Assessment</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item>Adenocarcinoma of the sigmoid colon (C18.7). Clinical stage T3 N1 on CT, mismatch-repair proficient, with a raised CEA. This is colon rather than rectal cancer and is not bulky or obstructing, so surgery first is standard; treatment before surgery is not needed. The final stage and the need for chemotherapy depend on the surgical pathology. [S]</item><item>Indeterminate 9 mm liver lesion. Too small to characterize on CT. A single liver metastasis would change the plan to a combined approach, so it should be clarified before the operation; a benign lesion such as a hemangioma or cyst is more probable with normal liver tests. [S]</item><item>Iron deficiency anemia (D50.0). Hemoglobin 10.3 g/dL with absent iron stores. Correcting it before surgery lowers the chance of transfusion; with surgery about two weeks away, intravenous iron is the practical route. [S]</item><item>Family history. A mother reported to have died of a similar bowel disorder, of unknown nature and age, is uncertain information. The tumor is mismatch-repair proficient, which makes Lynch syndrome improbable, but current guidance supports offering germline testing to people with colorectal cancer. [S]</item></list><paragraph>Data reviewed: Colonoscopy report and biopsy pathology with mismatch-repair immunohistochemistry (MLH1, PMS2, MSH2, MSH6 intact); CT of the chest, abdomen, and pelvis from eleven days ago; today&apos;s CEA 6.4 ng/mL, hemoglobin 10.3 g/dL, and normal liver tests; the presenting blood count and iron studies. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
</component>

<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.10" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="18776-5" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Plan of care note"/>
    <title>Plan of Treatment</title>
    <text><list><item>MRI of the liver with contrast before surgery to characterize the 9 mm lesion. [S]</item><item>If the liver lesion is benign, proceed to laparoscopic sigmoid colectomy with lymph node dissection by the colorectal surgery team; no treatment before surgery. [S]</item><item>Intravenous ferric carboxymaltose 750 mg now and again in one week, with a blood count and ferritin six to eight weeks later. [S]</item><item>After surgery: for low-risk stage III disease (T1 to T3 with one to three nodes), three months of CAPOX; for high-risk stage III disease, six months; for stage II disease without high-risk features, surveillance alone. [S]</item><item>Before any capecitabine, DPYD genotyping; check neuropathy at baseline and at every visit during and after oxaliplatin. [S]</item><item>Genetic counseling for germline testing after surgery. [S]</item><item>After treatment, surveillance by the CMID and NCCN schedule: CEA and examination every 3 to 6 months for two years, CT every 6 to 12 months for five years, and colonoscopy one year after resection. [S]</item><item>Discussed with Dr. Gallacher by telephone today and copied to the colorectal surgery team; the patient agrees with the plan and asked for the next steps in writing, which were given. [S]</item><item>Follow-up: Medical oncology will see him two to three weeks after surgery with the pathology to decide on adjuvant therapy, and will give any chemotherapy. Primary care continues to coordinate iron treatment, mood and distress screening, and survivorship care. [S]</item></list></text>

  </section>
